    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Let's get back to the nitty-gritty. As a solo freelancer without a business entity, you're indeed personally responsible for any losses or damages that might arise from your work. This means that if you're found liable for a breach of contract or negligence, you could be personally responsible for paying damages, including legal fees.\n\nIn terms of the scope of these \"losses,\" it really depends on the specific circumstances of the case. However, as a general rule, courts tend to limit the amount of damages that can be awarded to the amount of the defendant's (in this case, your) assets.\n\nFor example, if you're found liable for $100,000 in damages, the court might limit the award to $100,000, even if you have a larger net worth. This is because the court wants to ensure that you're not unfairly penalized for a single mistake or breach of contract.\n\nThat being said, there are some exceptions to this rule. For example, if you're found liable for reckless or willful misconduct, the court might not limit the award to the amount of your assets. In such cases, you could be personally responsible for paying the full amount of the damages, including any legal fees.\n\nIt's",
